#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119934 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62196ae9-df2e-5a3a-bc0a-1c0f4c40af3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Normal Vector To a Plane Given by Parametric Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20671737-03a7-5430-89bf-040b752377e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-4,3,31⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429805} {:answer/id #uuid "6c5b071d-27ac-57b7-af6d-728934e76426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,5,31⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429808} {:answer/id #uuid "745f1be0-1559-5f77-9632-8cdde3498554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-5,3,31⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429809} {:answer/id #uuid "cb172289-6398-5303-bbd8-83b5e2e9bc68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-5,7,31⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429811} {:answer/id #uuid "dea73573-73e0-539b-8d97-0a1d8ac2f0eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-5,3,27⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "745f1be0-1559-5f77-9632-8cdde3498554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-5,3,31⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429809} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9b496fd-8c3a-561b-bf53-11d88d290ff5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c3b23dc-4bcb-57b2-b0fc-dd67734d3e36" :question/math-academy-id "q-128493" :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 17 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+s[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨2,-7,1⟩$ and $w=⟨3,5,0⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-5i+3j+31k \\\\  & =⟨-5,3,31⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-5,3,31⟩.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119932 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3118d2af-771f-54c9-b18e-5196b87b6dca" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of an N-Dimensional Parallelepiped"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3194d732-4a27-57e4-90b5-376278b1fad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429806} {:answer/id #uuid "3c025708-e1f6-590a-bdc9-0995f51cbaec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429807} {:answer/id #uuid "a745ff62-0494-5386-bded-3bb0f59f39cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429810} {:answer/id #uuid "f1560774-e64f-5713-95be-58cfbace4e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429813} {:answer/id #uuid "fb522c79-1eec-53f4-a1ce-a41acd3ad978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1560774-e64f-5713-95be-58cfbace4e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429813} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b729052-de1a-56fa-9f79-cc8f868046e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d62e579-1b65-5746-996a-3e6e877f180c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158107" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 & 13 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 11 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 & 13 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 11 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=(-7)\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 1=-70.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-70|=70.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115356 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfa1654c-e948-5fc3-a89a-4fe52cc8fccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398578} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1b7514-50b8-5ddd-9dc3-5b90007cd7cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi n}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398579} {:answer/id #uuid "61124a49-e750-5eed-9f63-4fab70f48c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398580} {:answer/id #uuid "282a04d7-5121-553a-8c70-66602f8c83db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398581} {:answer/id #uuid "4a1c8270-8fd2-50fc-8447-30acc60e9312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa1654c-e948-5fc3-a89a-4fe52cc8fccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398578} :answer-field/id #uuid "4112b8f8-a1d3-5ca1-a272-50f00bc81e5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b2987e81-f85b-5262-9ea2-835ea44d86e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-162644" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=(t+1)\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t)\\,j+{e}^{-t}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\bbox[5pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAA}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $t+1$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t)$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\n3t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component ${e}^{-t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0777157f-29ea-5e24-9e8e-6b9a24e51747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481580} {:answer/id #uuid "0f1ed2e1-55cd-57e9-aa50-68794e5c6eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481584} {:answer/id #uuid "162d01d4-4c05-520f-a7ba-901b79767c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481588} {:answer/id #uuid "da46d0be-42c1-57a1-ab4a-e8a9512a5cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481634} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2dac8c-ea1a-540c-93ab-6f8d5b85be8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "162d01d4-4c05-520f-a7ba-901b79767c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481588} :answer-field/id #uuid "287256f7-b082-5a2a-854b-414b7b8a37a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34f7a331-3a09-5214-b793-a1233bb14dc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-162827" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.16$ | $0.09$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.44$ | $0.21$ | $0.05$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=0)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=0)=f(0,0)=0.09.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0)=0.3\\cdot 0.3=0.09.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-2) & =0.16\\ne 0.3\\cdot 0.6={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-2) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f001f66-21ad-5810-9a24-3727a01a58d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/95c7144f229cc34fcb98b1c1fa8bda8af8ca9dff68c7ae466e388ce3e525bc85.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285523} {:answer/id #uuid "5829deab-1ed2-5ddf-959a-31d73972126d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ec/eceb9c773d52b8bae73837252a885258b5a9b735cf3f24954b8755049d7cf07d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285596} {:answer/id #uuid "64ba1242-9f38-5065-b2a2-156c1dffa463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/89/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285703} {:answer/id #uuid "6e22185e-2dca-5e48-a97a-c408b9904280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/45/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285800} {:answer/id #uuid "97b4fd05-3101-5cc2-857b-8c7b3c842a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/43/43f665cebf4c3af1f1178d94b4ceec5d60bcfd4e61ebf955ed43cb603f8232b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e22185e-2dca-5e48-a97a-c408b9904280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/45/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285800} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ea3661b-4d1c-56cf-956a-8769e8d2e497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5558c8b0-025f-54bf-b54a-ad58d686884f" :question/math-academy-id "q-181770" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=Z\\times R$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times R\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is an integer number and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/3f/3fdfed680e3fb46adbe8a70aac86682aacb85d43e0675923f87658635fc89a1e.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number.\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/7f/7fbda500ecca7e18c9db9939ad78d4dcc6bf488bc7fc4de1137e764ce21a0254.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times R$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/45/4535a7c6aa7fe8f358d3463b4654c735e53df373d1001916dc035290ca54f969.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78d7a514-ecfa-55cb-87c6-b60de1c022f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448708} {:answer/id #uuid "dc689acd-0734-5dcf-80c8-9704924c5f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc689acd-0734-5dcf-80c8-9704924c5f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448709} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5b60060-524b-5cf4-87fe-091fb1042d78" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449394} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eb89f53-77c8-596d-ae53-cd6a097dbbf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448710} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7743f7-78bf-58ff-9155-635e6f903288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eb89f53-77c8-596d-ae53-cd6a097dbbf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448710} :answer-field/id #uuid "13793ac0-8ac1-549e-a713-98a29ed44f94" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449395} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a43a32d-e760-5c98-a4e4-877d467394b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448712} {:answer/id #uuid "0a70e804-1854-50d9-892f-b441bea742d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a43a32d-e760-5c98-a4e4-877d467394b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448712} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f8b7059-0041-56e3-a03b-3f0d2038120d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07b54935-f6c1-5cc5-a220-23bab58f39a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-47653" :question/problem "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n\n- **II.** $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n\n- **III.** The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$ intersect at infinitely many points" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot k-2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6k+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $6k+6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a04b551f-3323-5b91-b01e-941912cc92be" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies on a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2486b2f1-5c38-55cd-a205-21e202e09c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339446} {:answer/id #uuid "2cadd916-ed59-5b50-9446-268229ac2732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339516} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc45ccf-adc7-53ad-a852-af95cfc40061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$u$ does not lie on $Π$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339825} {:answer/id #uuid "733ba44c-1a53-5f59-af78-2d354dda43f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340150} {:answer/id #uuid "7caedebc-c212-5602-b507-20831a0f0c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cadd916-ed59-5b50-9446-268229ac2732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339516} :answer-field/id #uuid "520ff562-4653-55df-b4dc-8fbcaed97a63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6c3f74b-5a27-519e-8d22-1736702759dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-74599" :question/problem "Determine if the vector $u$ lies on the plane $Π:r=sb+tc$, $t,s\\in R$, where  $b=⟨-1,4,3⟩$, $c=⟨1,-6,-7⟩$, and  $u=⟨1,-10,-15⟩.$ In the affirmative case, find the value of $s\\cdot t$ from the decomposition $u=sb+tc.$" :question/worked-solution "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+t=1 \\\\ 4s-6t=-10 \\\\ 3s-7t=-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -6 & -10 \\\\ 3 & -7 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 3 & -7 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+t=1 \\\\ -2t=-6 \\\\ 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2t=-6\\,⟹\\,t=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s+t & =1 \\\\ -s+3 & =1 \\\\ -s & =-2 \\\\ s & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=2,\\,t=3,$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=2b+3c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=2\\cdot 3=6.$"}]}
